<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<opml version="2.0">
  <head>
    <title>The Great Convergence — Why AI Software Development, Why Now</title>
    <ownerName>Matt Weber</ownerName>
    <ownerEmail>golfergeek@gmail.com</ownerEmail>
  </head>
  <body>
    <outline text="The Great Convergence — Why AI Software Development, Why Now" _note="Opening teaching map for Matt Weber. “The Great Convergence” is Matt&apos;s framing for the forces changing software development: capable models, agent harnesses, faster development cadence, and organizations reclaiming software that fits how they actually work.">
      <outline text="Welcome — I am Matt Weber" _note="Senior developer for a few decades; recent Angular and C# backend work; builder of open-source platform tools for exercising AI-development learning; now fascinated by teaching it. Contact: golfergeek@gmail.com.">
        <outline text="A few decades as a senior developer"/>
        <outline text="Recent Angular frontend and C# backend work"/>
        <outline text="Discovered AI software development"/>
        <outline text="Built open-source platforms as a learning laboratory"/>
        <outline text="Now focused on how to teach the operating model"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Why software became the first proving ground" _note="Code offers enormous training material and unusually fast evidence through compilers, tests, linters, and observable behavior. It is highly testable, not magically simple or perfectly deterministic.">
        <outline text="1 — Code completion"/>
        <outline text="2 — Code generation"/>
        <outline text="3 — Agent harnesses"/>
        <outline text="The bottleneck moves from typing toward definition and review"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="What a harness adds" _note="The harness turns model capability into an operating loop. Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, and other surfaces combine these elements differently.">
        <outline text="Prompt — the immediate request"/>
        <outline text="Context — repository, instructions, history, and knowledge"/>
        <outline text="Tools — files, terminal, browser, APIs, and other agents"/>
        <outline text="LLM — reasoning and generation"/>
        <outline text="Agent loop — act, observe evidence, and iterate"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="The four forces converging">
        <outline text="Capable foundation models"/>
        <outline text="Operational IDE and CLI harnesses"/>
        <outline text="Radically faster development cadence"/>
        <outline text="Companies need software that fits their real work"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="This requires a mind shift—and it is not for everybody" _note="The convergence creates an opportunity, not an obligation. “Not now” can be responsible. High-assurance systems can still use AI for research, tests, review, and bounded proposals while preserving line-by-line human authority.">
        <outline text="Some organizations are not ready right now"/>
        <outline text="Some codebases demand exceptional assurance"/>
        <outline text="Adoption is a continuum, not an on/off switch"/>
        <outline text="Expand agent authority only when evidence supports it"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="For now — business-centered development" _note="Use verified cadence to address neglected business friction, then pursue new value. AI changes the build-versus-buy boundary without making every SaaS product obsolete.">
        <outline text="Own the ERP, SaaS, spreadsheet, and workflow gaps that matter"/>
        <outline text="Work down the long-neglected backlog"/>
        <outline text="Internal projects"/>
        <outline text="Customer-facing projects"/>
        <outline text="Expansion projects and new value"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Cadence changes the developer&apos;s job" _note="Cadence is the sustainable rate of moving from intention to trustworthy evidence. The developer increasingly manages outcomes, boundaries, contracts, authority, integration, and proof—not merely lines of code.">
        <outline text="Coordinate parallel agents and codebases"/>
        <outline text="Specifications become living instruments"/>
        <outline text="Short loop — define → delegate → inspect → learn"/>
        <outline text="Meetings move from handoffs toward decisions and risk"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Speed requires a stronger system" _note="Fast generation amplifies both strong definition and weak assumptions. Agent training means maintaining the agent&apos;s operating environment: role, context, memory, standards, tools, permissions, examples, and evaluations.">
        <outline text="Guardrails and authority boundaries"/>
        <outline text="Governance, ownership, and review dates"/>
        <outline text="Process, independent evidence, and rollback"/>
        <outline text="Project and company memory"/>
        <outline text="Coding and architectural standards"/>
        <outline text="Maintained skills, agents, tests, and evaluations"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Adoption requires comfort with continuing change" _note="An organization may freeze a good-enough system, but that consciously trades away some future improvements. Benefiting next week, month, and year requires controlled review, experiments, upgrades, and retirement without creating novelty churn.">
        <outline text="Models, harnesses, providers, and tactics keep moving"/>
        <outline text="Separate durable principles from changing terrain"/>
        <outline text="Budget for controlled modernization"/>
        <outline text="This commitment is genuinely challenging"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="The organization must learn to build software again" _note="Reclaiming software also reclaims responsibilities that vendors absorbed. Generation without product ownership, testing, operations, and maintenance creates a fragile portfolio.">
        <outline text="Prioritize a real project portfolio"/>
        <outline text="Restore acceptance, unit, integration, browser, and security testing"/>
        <outline text="Own deployment, observability, support, and rollback"/>
        <outline text="Fund maintenance and lifecycle ownership"/>
        <outline text="Retire software that no longer earns its cost"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Learn the room before teaching the room" _note="Use the opening for a short, sanitized conversation. The detailed and mixed-client-safe worksheet is docs/instructor/COHORT-DISCOVERY.md.">
        <outline text="What kind of organization and customers?"/>
        <outline text="How many developers, roles, and experience levels?"/>
        <outline text="Excited, curious, skeptical, anxious, or mandated?"/>
        <outline text="What stack, delivery cadence, and testing maturity?"/>
        <outline text="What opportunities and high-assurance boundaries?"/>
        <outline text="What would make Friday and the next 90 days successful?"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="The Great Convergence" _note="Capable models + operational harnesses + faster verified cadence + the need for software that fits. The opportunity is not simply more code; it is a new operating model for how an organization decides, builds, verifies, learns, and owns software.">
        <outline text="Define well"/>
        <outline text="Build fast"/>
        <outline text="Verify hard"/>
        <outline text="Govern and maintain the system"/>
        <outline text="The rest of the week teaches responsible operation"/>
      </outline>
    </outline>
  </body>
</opml>
